{"records":[{"id":"9rbyzvt8pb","title":"SAFB restricts contact domain boundaries associated with L1 chimeric transcription","description":"Long interspersed element-1 (LINE-1 or L1) comprises 17% of the human genome, continuously generates genetic variations, and cause disease in certain cases. However, the regulation and function of L1 remain poorly understood. Here, we uncover that L1 can enrich RNA polymerase IIs (Pol IIs), express L1 chimeric transcripts and create contact domain boundaries. This impact of L1 is restricted by a nuclear matrix protein Scaffold Attachment Factor B (SAFB) that recognizes transcriptionally active L1s by binding L1 transcripts to inhibit Pol II enrichment and reduce L1 transcription. Acute inhibition of Pol II transcription abolishes the domain boundaries associated with L1 chimeric transcripts, indicating a transcription-dependent mechanism. Deleting L1 impairs domain boundary formation, and L1 insertions during evolution have introduced species-specific domain boundaries. Our data show that L1 can create Pol II enriched regions that alter genome organization, and that SAFB regulates L1 and Pol II activity to preserve gene regulation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-25T05:11:01.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9rbyzvt8pb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9rbyzvt8pb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yaqiang","last_name":"Hong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"56sxpgs4d9","title":"Comparison of L1 insertions in hg19 with six other mammalian genomes","description":"The table contains the position and result of cross-species comparison for all RepeatMasker annotated L1 elements in hg19. File is gzip'ped. The first eleven columns correspond to the information provided by RepeatMasker. Then 4 columns provide annotation of each L1 as used in the associated publication. The remaining columns refer to the result from liftover of the L1 to the respective genome (hg38, gorGor5, rheMac8, mm10, rn6, bosTau8, canFam3), and consists of one of four categories: absent, degenerate, notLINE, or present. Degenerate means the L1 was lifted to the genome, but was heavily truncated (size less than 33% of human). notLINE means the L1 was lifted but the liftover position is not recognised as a LINE repeat by RepeatMasker in the new genome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-04-21T12:15:58.071Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/56sxpgs4d9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/56sxpgs4d9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Attig","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"trfngprnv4","title":"PDS5 proteins control genome architecture by limiting the lifetime of cohesin-NIPBL complexes","description":"Cohesin-NIPBL complexes extrude genomic DNA into loops that are constrained by CTCF boundaries. This process has important regulatory functions and weakens the separation between euchromatic and heterochromatic compartments. Cohesin can also bind PDS5A or PDS5B, which do not support loop extrusion but are required for the formation of CTCF boundaries. How PDS5 proteins perform this function is unknown. Here we show by in vitro single-molecule imaging that PDS5 proteins stop loop extrusion by facilitating the dissociation of NIPBL from cohesin. Hi-C experiments suggest that this function is required for the establishment of CTCF boundaries in cells. In silico modelling indicates that PDS5 proteins enable the separation between compartments by limiting cohesin’s velocity and chromatin-residence time. The degree of this compartmentalization depends on the frequency with which chromatin is extruded relative to the time it takes for compartments to form. These results identify PDS5 proteins as key regulators of genome organization.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-25T02:52:14.64Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/trfngprnv4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/trfngprnv4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gordana","last_name":"Wutz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Iain Finley","last_name":"Davidson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edward J.","last_name":"Banigan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Roman R","last_name":"Stocsits","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ryotaro ","last_name":"Kawasumi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wen","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kota","last_name":"Nagasaka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lorenzo ","last_name":"Costantino","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ralf ","last_name":"Jansen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kouji","last_name":"Hirota","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dana","last_name":"Branzei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Leonid A","last_name":"Mirny","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jan-Michael","last_name":"Peters","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Chromatin","Computational Bioinformatics","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x5m39wbkr5","title":"Data for: Of False Friends and Familiar Foes: Comparing native and non-native understanding of figurative phrases","description":"Rating data for English native speakers, general non-natives (various L1s) and Chinese L1 speakers, rating figurative phrases (in English) for familiarity, transparency, meaning and decomposability. Phrases are English idioms, novel metaphors, unfamiliar idioms (translated from German and Bulgarian) and idioms translated from Chinese. Non-native speakers include demographic data about their language learning background, English usage and vocabulary proficiency. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-11-20T21:13:44.558Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x5m39wbkr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x5m39wbkr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gareth","last_name":"Carrol","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jeannette","last_name":"Littlemore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Margaret","last_name":"Dowens","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Linguistics","Applied Linguistics","Psycholinguistics","Cognitive Linguistics"],"journal_issn":["00243841"]}],"total_records":137747,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":151,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":137747,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":137081,"restricted":68,"embargoed":598},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2276},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1340},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":678},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":471},{"id":"https://ro